// Copyright (c) 2026 Jurjen Stellingwerff
// SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-3.0-or-later
//
// A PLACE THAT OWNS MOBS — and the BOUND that is the point of one.
//
// `plans/30` R5, `@X301` and `@FR-E-Poi-Owns`.  Owner, 2026-08-28:
// *"so a given scenario has a set of points of interest and these have
// their attached mobs to them with their cycle that can be quite far and
// elaborate."*
//
// ⚠⚠ **A POI is the scenario-scale object the economy's node is not.**
// [`ROBOT_ECONOMY.md`](../docs/ROBOT_ECONOMY.md)'s node is a fact about a
// 1.5 km cell — *this cell mines*.  A point of interest is its **local
// expression on a patch**: the working face, the spoil heap, the
// maintenance shed, the nest.  ⚠ So a base does not have to land on a
// mine to have something to look at — it lands in a neighbourhood, and
// the neighbourhood has features.
//
// ── ⚠⚠ WHY IT EXISTS AT ALL, AND IT IS NOT THE RECORD ───────────
//
// ⚠⚠ **The record is scaffolding; the BOUND is the phase.**  `@X299`
// needs *could this ever be in this window?* answered **statically**, so
// that a mob nobody can see costs nothing and
// [`plans/22`](../plans/22-the-field-cache/README.md)'s LOD refusal needs
// no exception.  A bound per MOB would be `N` queries; `@FR-E-Poi-Owns`
// is what collapses it:
//
// > ⚠⚠ **the POI IS the bound, and culling one culls its whole
// > population** — one query instead of `N`.
//
// ⚠⚠ **AND THE POPULATION'S SIZE DOES NOT ENTER IT.**  A POI with forty
// haulers on one route has exactly the bound of a POI with one, because
// a bound is a union over ROUTES and never over BODIES.  That is what
// makes the collapse real rather than a change of index: `poi_bound`
// reads `PoiRoute`s and cannot see a body at all.
//
// ── ⚠⚠ THE BOUND, AND ITS PROOF IS A COROLLARY OF `@X336` ───────
//
// > ⚠⚠ **The bound is the union, over the cycle's legs, of the disc
// > centred on each leg's anchor with that leg's LENGTH as its radius.**
//
// ⚠ It is not a new claim and it needs no new measurement — it falls out
// of two things already written down:
//
//   1. **`@FR-E-Non-Increasing`** — a mob's distance to its current
//      destination never increases; `flow_steps` strictly decreases it
//      and `flow_sidesteps` holds it equal, and `errand_descend` reaches
//      for those two and nothing else.  So a body that STARTS leg `i` at
//      field distance `lengths[i]` from `anchors[i]` is inside
//      `disc(anchors[i], lengths[i])` for every moment of that leg,
//      deviating or not.
//   2. **`@X336`** — what is TOTAL about a mob is its DISTANCE: a body's
//      field distance to its current anchor equals the rule's under
//      every deviation.  The rule's distance inside leg `i` is at most
//      `lengths[i]`, so the body's is too.
//
// ⚠⚠ **So containment is a COROLLARY rather than a hypothesis**, and the
// gate exists to catch the day one of those two stops being true —
// `plans/30` § Count the RE-ASSERTION SITES, with the sidestep at
// number 3.
//
// ── ⚠⚠ AND THE CLAIM IS PER-LEG; THE UNION IS ONLY THE QUERY ────
//
// ⚠⚠ **A UNION IS SLACK, AND ASKING IT IS NOT ASKING ANYTHING.**  The
// two facts above are about **one leg at a time**, and the union of the
// discs is a much larger promise than either of them makes.  R5 measured
// what that costs a gate: containment *against the union* survived a
// radius **one hex short**, a **terminal leg given no disc at all**, and
// a **rim excluded by an off-by-one** — three defects, none visible,
// because a hex outside its own leg's disc is inside a neighbouring
// leg's.
//
// ⚠ So there are two doors and the difference between them is nameable
// (`@FR-F-Nameable-Difference`):
//
//   * **`bound_disc_holds(b, leg, h)`** — *is a body walking THIS leg
//     where the rule allows?*  This is the CLAIM, and it is what a gate
//     must ask.
//   * **`bound_holds(b, h)` / `bound_meets(b, …)`** — *could anything
//     bounded by this be there?*  This is the QUERY, and slack is exactly
//     what it wants: over-answering costs work, under-answering is a
//     defect.
//
// ── ⚠⚠ IT IS READ IN LATTICE DISTANCE, AND THAT IS A CHOICE ─────
//
// ⚠ The region above is stated in FIELD distance, which is where
// `@FR-E-Non-Increasing` proves it.  `bound_holds` asks `lat_distance`
// instead, and the two are not the same set:
//
// > ⚠⚠ **`lat_distance(a, b)` is at most the length of ANY path from
// > `a` to `b`**, so it is at most the field distance — and the lattice
// > disc therefore **CONTAINS** the field disc.
//
// ⚠ A superset is the safe direction and the only safe direction.  The
// query's job (R6) is *may I skip materialising this population?*: a
// bound that answers *maybe* where the truth is *no* costs work, and one
// that answered *no* where the truth is *yes* would put a body somewhere
// the rule never said.  ⚠⚠ **And what it buys is that the bound needs no
// FIELD to be read** — it is `2 × legs` integers, static for the sortie,
// answerable with no world at all, which is what `@X300`'s *a query
// rather than a scan* has to mean if it is to mean anything.
//
// ⚠ What the looseness COSTS is measured rather than assumed —
// `tests/30_r5_the_bound.loft` § PROBE counts both regions against the
// three authored maps.
//
// ── ⚠⚠ STATE BELONGS TO THE PLACE, AND THE BOUND DOES NOT MOVE ──
//
// `@FR-E-Place-State`: a POI may hold as much state as it likes — there
// are few of them and they do not move — while a mob may hold none
// beyond `carry` and `slip`.  ⚠⚠ **And the bound is INVARIANT under a
// state change**, which is what lets a POI be stateful for free: every
// cycle a POI can issue is anchored on that POI, so the union over its
// states is the same region.  ⚠ `poi_state_set` writes `since` with the
// state because the closed form becomes **piecewise** at a state change
// and a segment with no `t0` is `@X335`'s leg boundary with a new
// subject.
//
// ⚠⚠ **A POI IS NEVER CULLED** (`@FR-E-Poi-Persists`, `@X304`) — which
// is why there is no verb here that removes one.  A collapsed mine is a
// STATE: the workers still walk to it, arrive, and find out.  *Not
// materialised* and *culled* are different words and this file only ever
// means the first.
//
// ── ⚠ WHAT LANDS INERT ──────────────────────────────────────────
//
// ⚠ `POI_KIND_NONE` is 0 and `poi_new()` is one, so [loft#914]'s silent
// default is a place that is not a place — and nothing in the game
// builds a `PoiWorld` yet, exactly as `ROLE_NONE` is what every
// `Enemy { … }` literal already means.

use lattice;
use flow;
use painted;
use palette;
use passable;
use height;
use errand;
use spawn;
use fixstep;

// ─── ⚠ WHAT KIND OF PLACE ───────────────────────────────────────

// ⚠ `POI_KIND_NONE` is 0 for [loft#914]'s reason: a partial literal is
// then a place with no population and no bound, which is today's game.
pub const POI_KIND_NONE:  integer = 0;
pub const POI_KIND_NEST:  integer = 1;   // where a gatherer's round ends
pub const POI_KIND_FACE:  integer = 2;   // the working face — what is cut
pub const POI_KIND_DEPOT: integer = 3;   // the drop-off a hauler runs to
pub const POI_KIND_SHED:  integer = 4;   // the maintenance point (`@X339`)
pub const POI_KIND_HEAP:  integer = 5;   // the spoil heap, the wreck field
pub const POI_KIND_COUNT: integer = 6;

// What a POI IS, to the mobs attached to it.
//
// ⚠⚠ **A KIND IS A ROW AND THE TABLE IS INDEXED** — never a `kind ==`
// anywhere, which is `@X333`'s rule with places as its second subject.
// ⚠ The one column that matters is `anchor`: **which of its population's
// three anchors this POI is standing on**.  That is the whole of
// *anchors derive from the POI*, and it is data.
pub struct PoiKind {
    name:   text,
    anchor: integer,
}

pub fn poi_kind_row(name: text, anchor: integer) -> PoiKind {
    PoiKind { name: name, anchor: anchor }
}

// ⚠⚠ **THE KIND TABLE.**  A kind the engine does not know reads as
// `POI_KIND_NONE` rather than as a branch nobody took.
pub fn poi_kinds() -> vector<PoiKind> {
    pk_all: vector<PoiKind> = [];
    pk_all += [poi_kind_row("none",  ANCHOR_NONE)];
    pk_all += [poi_kind_row("nest",  ANCHOR_HOME)];
    pk_all += [poi_kind_row("face",  ANCHOR_WORK)];
    pk_all += [poi_kind_row("depot", ANCHOR_ALT)];
    // ⚠ A shed is a NEST by geometry and not by fiction — both are the
    // anchor a round ENDS at (`@FR-E-Home-Is-A-Place`), and what
    // separates them is which role attaches, which is the route's column
    // rather than this one's.
    pk_all += [poi_kind_row("shed",  ANCHOR_HOME)];
    pk_all += [poi_kind_row("heap",  ANCHOR_ALT)];
    pk_all
}

// The row for a kind — `POI_KIND_NONE`'s for anything the table has not
// got.  ⚠ It is the ONE lookup; nothing else may index the table.
pub fn poi_kind_of(kind: integer) -> PoiKind {
    pko_all = poi_kinds();
    pko_i   = kind;
    if pko_i < 0 || pko_i >= len(pko_all) { pko_i = POI_KIND_NONE; }
    pko_all[pko_i] ?? poi_kind_row("none", ANCHOR_NONE)
}

// The kind a script or a measurement spells `name`, or `POI_KIND_NONE`.
//
// ⚠ **The writer and the reader are ONE** (`@D007` is what happens when
// they are two): a name comes out of the row it names.
pub fn poi_kind_named(name: text) -> integer {
    pkn_i = 0;
    for pkn_row in poi_kinds() {
        if pkn_row.name == name { return pkn_i; }
        pkn_i += 1;
    }
    POI_KIND_NONE
}

// ─── ⚠ WHAT STATE IT IS IN ──────────────────────────────────────

// ⚠ A state a mob can SEE on arrival, never a number on a screen
// (`@X304`).  ⚠⚠ `WORKING` is 0 so a partial literal is a place that is
// fine, which is every POI a scenario has ever authored.
pub const POI_STATE_WORKING:   integer = 0;
pub const POI_STATE_DAMAGED:   integer = 1;
pub const POI_STATE_COLLAPSED: integer = 2;
pub const POI_STATE_RAIDED:    integer = 3;
pub const POI_STATE_REPAIRED:  integer = 4;
pub const POI_STATE_COUNT:     integer = 5;

// ─── ⚠⚠ THE PLACE ITSELF ────────────────────────────────────────

// Four facts and a timestamp — and the timestamp is the one that is not
// obvious.
//
// ⚠⚠ **`since` is what makes the closed form PIECEWISE rather than
// broken.**  `position(t) = cycle(poi.state, anchors, t − slip)` gains an
// argument the moment a POI can change state, and a segment with no `t0`
// is a cycle evaluated against a rule that was not in force — which is
// `@X335`'s *the bank does not restart at a leg boundary* asked about a
// place instead of a leg.  ⚠ It is one field on a POI and **nothing at
// all on a mob**, which is `@FR-E-Place-State` paying for itself.
pub struct Poi {
    kind:  integer,
    q:     integer,
    r:     integer,
    state: integer,
    since: integer,   // base units at which the current state began
}

// A place that is not a place — which is every scenario today.
pub fn poi_new() -> Poi {
    Poi { kind: POI_KIND_NONE, q: 0, r: 0,
          state: POI_STATE_WORKING, since: 0 }
}

// A place, spelled out.  ⚠ Build from here or from `poi_new` and never
// from a partial literal — [loft#914] would take a default silently and
// put the nest on the origin.
pub fn poi_at(kind: integer, q: integer, r: integer) -> Poi {
    Poi { kind: kind, q: q, r: r,
          state: POI_STATE_WORKING, since: 0 }
}

// Where this place is.
pub fn poi_hex(p: const Poi) -> Hex {
    Hex { q: p.q, r: p.r }
}

// Is there a place here at all?
pub fn poi_real(p: const Poi) -> boolean {
    poi_kind_of(p.kind).anchor != ANCHOR_NONE
}

// The player raided it, or the swarm repaired it.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THE ONE DOOR FOR A STATE CHANGE, and it writes BOTH halves** —
// the pattern this repo keeps six times over (`break_structure` is *"the
// one site, and it does both halves"*).  A state written without its
// `since` is a new segment of a piecewise form with no `t0`, and that
// fails **silently**: the mobs keep walking, to the rule that is no
// longer in force.
//
// Example: @DRY-187
pub fn poi_state_set(p: Poi, state: integer, now: integer) {
    p.state = state;
    p.since = now;
}

// ─── ⚠⚠ THE POPULATION — a set of ROUTES, never a list of BODIES ──

// One route a POI issues, and how many mobs are on it.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THE POPULATION IS A ROUTE AND A COUNT, and that is what makes the
// bound cheap.**  Forty haulers walking one route have one bound between
// them; a list of forty bodies would have forty and would move every
// tick.  ⚠ `count` is therefore read by the MATERIALISER (R6) and by
// nothing in this file — `poi_bound` cannot see it, which is the claim
// `tests/30_r5_the_bound.loft` gates.
//
// ⚠ `a` and `b` are the anchors the POI is NOT: a role uses at most
// three and the POI supplies one, so two is exactly enough.  Which of
// them lands where is decided by the ROLE's row and the KIND's row
// together — see `poi_errand` — and never by their order here.
pub struct PoiRoute {
    poi:   integer,   // index into `PoiWorld.pois`
    role:  integer,   // an `errand.loft` role — the round it walks
    kind:  u8,        // the mover class, which is what decides passability
    a:     Hex,
    b:     Hex,
    count: integer,   // how many mobs are on this route
}

pub fn poi_route(poi: integer, role: integer, kind: u8,
                 a: const Hex, b: const Hex, count: integer) -> PoiRoute {
    PoiRoute { poi: poi, role: role, kind: kind,
               a: Hex { q: a.q, r: a.r }, b: Hex { q: b.q, r: b.r },
               count: count }
}

// ─── ⚠⚠ WHAT AN UN-MATERIALISED MOB IS ─────────────────────────

// One mob of a POI's population — materialised or not.
//
// ⚠⚠ **FOUR FIELDS, AND ONLY ONE OF THEM ACCUMULATES.**  `@X299` needs
// an un-tracked mob to be **COMPUTED and not simulated coarsely**, so
// everything about it that CAN be a function of `t` is one:
//
// | | where it comes from |
// |---|---|
// | which round it walks | `route` — an index, fixed for the sortie |
// | where on that round it started | `seat` — its place in the rota, and the offset is DERIVED from it (`poi_seat_offset`) |
// | which hex it is on now | `cycle_at`, at its own clock |
// | what is in its bag | ⚠⚠ **derived from the leg** — `errand_bag_for`, never stored |
// | how late it is | `slip`, and this is the ONLY thing a body can add |
// | whether its round has ended | `gone` |
//
// ⚠⚠ **`slip` IS WHY THE RECORD EXISTS AT ALL.**  Everything else could
// be recomputed from the route and the seat, so a mob nobody has ever
// looked at needs no memory whatever.  A mob that HAS had a body may
// have been pushed, and `@FR-E-Slip` says a deviation costs TIME and
// never DESTINATION — so throwing `slip` away when the body goes would
// **refund the delay**, and the mob would arrive as though nothing had
// been in its way.  ⚠ That is invisible from outside the window by
// construction, which is exactly why it has to be written down rather
// than argued about.
//
// ⚠ `gone` is the R4 ending reaching a mob with no body
// (`@FR-E-Home-Is-A-Place`).  A round ends on the CLOCK, not on being
// watched: a population whose far half worked for ever while its near
// half retired would break the equality R6b gates, and the ending is
// computable — `errand_home_done` asks the row, the routine, the rate
// and a hex, and never a roster.
pub struct PoiMob {
    route: integer,   // index into `PoiWorld.routes`
    seat:  integer,   // its place in the rota — 0 .. count-1
    slip:  integer,   // base units it has fallen behind (`@FR-E-Slip`)
    gone:  boolean,   // its round has ended, and it never comes back

    // ⚠⚠ **DID WE GIVE THIS MOB A BODY, and it is the one MIRROR on this
    // record** (`plans/30` R6b).  Who has a body is otherwise read off
    // the ROSTER — sweep it for `route.mob` — which is why nothing has to
    // be kept in step when `wave_deaths` compacts it.
    //
    // ⚠ **What the mirror is FOR is the tick a body VANISHES in.**  The
    // roster says *there is no body with this index*, and that means two
    // opposite things: *we never made one* and *the one we made has been
    // killed, or the bubble took it*.  Only this flag tells them apart,
    // and getting it wrong is a RESURRECTION — a POI quietly making a
    // second copy of a robot the player watched die.
    live:  boolean,
}

pub fn poi_mob(route: integer, seat: integer) -> PoiMob {
    PoiMob { route: route, seat: seat, slip: 0, gone: false, live: false }
}

// A scenario's places, and what walks between them.
//
// ⚠⚠ **FLAT, and the flatness is load-bearing** ([loft#974]): a
// `vector<PoiRoute>` field on `Poi` is the shape that reads back EMPTY
// once an unrelated struct has been allocated — the same reason `Cycle`
// is a vector of hexes rather than a vector of legs.  Each route carries
// its POI's index instead.
pub struct PoiWorld {
    pois:   vector<Poi>,
    routes: vector<PoiRoute>,
    mobs:   vector<PoiMob>,
}

// No places at all — which is every scenario today.
pub fn poi_world_new() -> PoiWorld {
    PoiWorld { pois: [], routes: [], mobs: [] }
}

// The route at `i`, or a route that runs nothing.  ⚠ The ONE lookup.
pub fn poi_route_of(w: const PoiWorld, i: integer) -> PoiRoute {
    if i < 0 || i >= len(w.routes) {
        return poi_route(0, ROLE_NONE, 0 as u8? ?? 0, Hex { q: 0, r: 0 },
                         Hex { q: 0, r: 0 }, 0);
    }
    prf_r = w.routes[i];
    if prf_r == null {
        return poi_route(0, ROLE_NONE, 0 as u8? ?? 0, Hex { q: 0, r: 0 },
                         Hex { q: 0, r: 0 }, 0);
    }
    poi_route(prf_r.poi, prf_r.role, prf_r.kind, prf_r.a, prf_r.b,
              prf_r.count)
}

// The place at `i`, or a place that is not one.  ⚠ The ONE lookup.
pub fn poi_of(w: const PoiWorld, i: integer) -> Poi {
    if i < 0 || i >= len(w.pois) { return poi_new(); }
    pof_p = w.pois[i];
    if pof_p == null { return poi_new(); }
    Poi { kind: pof_p.kind, q: pof_p.q, r: pof_p.r,
          state: pof_p.state, since: pof_p.since }
}

// ─── ⚠⚠ ANCHORS DERIVE FROM THE POI ─────────────────────────────

// Does this role's round use this anchor at all?
//
// ⚠ Asked of the ROW and never of the role id, so a role added to
// `errand_roles()` is answered here the day it exists.  ⚠⚠ The terminal
// leg counts: a harvester's `home` is an anchor its round reaches once
// (`@X341`), and a bound that left it out would not contain the walk
// that ends the round.
pub fn poi_role_uses(row: const Role, anchor: integer) -> boolean {
    if anchor == ANCHOR_NONE { return false; }
    if row.empty == anchor || row.laden == anchor { return true; }
    errand_terminal(row) && anchor == ANCHOR_HOME
}

// The routine a mob on this route runs.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THIS IS *anchors derive from the POI*, and it is the whole of the
// attachment.**  The POI's hex goes into the anchor its KIND names; the
// anchors the role uses and the POI is not are filled from the route's
// `a` then `b`, **in anchor order** — HOME, WORK, ALT — so the mapping is
// a fact about the two tables and never about how a caller happened to
// order two hexes.
//
// ⚠ An anchor the role does not use is left at the origin, where
// `errand_destination_of` will never ask for it.
//
// Example: @DRY-188
pub fn poi_errand(p: const Poi, rt: const PoiRoute) -> Errand {
    per_row  = errand_role_of(rt.role);
    per_mine = poi_kind_of(p.kind).anchor;
    per_next = 0;
    per_h = Hex { q: 0, r: 0 };
    per_w = Hex { q: 0, r: 0 };
    per_a = Hex { q: 0, r: 0 };
    for per_k in [ANCHOR_HOME, ANCHOR_WORK, ANCHOR_ALT] {
        if !poi_role_uses(per_row, per_k) { continue; }
        per_at = Hex { q: p.q, r: p.r };
        if per_k != per_mine {
            // ⚠ Read the FIELDS and never the struct — `[rt.a, rt.b]`
            // is a copy of `PoiRoute` twice over (`advice[avoidable-copy]`),
            // and the two hexes are rebuilt here in any case.
            per_at = Hex { q: rt.a.q, r: rt.a.r };
            if per_next == 1 { per_at = Hex { q: rt.b.q, r: rt.b.r }; }
            if per_next > 1  { per_at = Hex { q: 0, r: 0 }; }
            per_next += 1;
        }
        if per_k == ANCHOR_HOME { per_h = per_at; }
        if per_k == ANCHOR_WORK { per_w = per_at; }
        if per_k == ANCHOR_ALT  { per_a = per_at; }
    }
    errand_at(rt.role, per_h, per_w, per_a)
}

// ─── ⚠⚠ THE BOUND ───────────────────────────────────────────────

// A static region, as discs.
//
// ⚠⚠ **`2 × legs` integers and no world**, which is what makes the query
// a query.  ⚠ FLAT for [loft#974]'s reason, like `Cycle` and `PoiWorld`
// above.
pub struct Bound {
    centres: vector<Hex>,
    radii:   vector<integer>,
}

// A region containing NOTHING — which is what a POI with no population
// bounds, and it is the honest answer rather than the whole plane.
pub fn bound_new() -> Bound {
    Bound { centres: [], radii: [] }
}

// How many discs — the query's cost, and `@FR-E-Closed-Form`'s O(legs)
// with the same legs in it.
pub fn bound_legs(b: const Bound) -> integer {
    len(b.radii)
}

// Does this region contain nothing at all?
pub fn bound_empty(b: const Bound) -> boolean {
    len(b.radii) == 0
}

// One more disc.  ⚠ `b` is a PARAMETER and therefore aliases — a `Bound`
// returned from a function would be a copy and the append a silent
// no-op ([loft#894]).
//
// ⚠⚠ **A NEGATIVE RADIUS IS CLAMPED AND NEVER DROPPED.**  Dropping would
// shift every disc after it, and `bound_disc_holds` is asked BY LEG
// INDEX — so a skipped disc would silently point the claim at a
// neighbouring leg's region and read green.  A radius of 0 is a disc
// holding exactly its centre, which is the honest answer.
pub fn bound_disc(b: Bound, centre: const Hex, radius: integer) {
    bdc_r = radius;
    if bdc_r < 0 { bdc_r = 0; }
    b.centres += [Hex { q: centre.q, r: centre.r }];
    b.radii   += [bdc_r];
}

// Everything in `other` as well.  ⚠⚠ **A union of discs is a LIST of
// discs and never a merge** — two discs that overlap are cheaper to keep
// apart than to reason about, and `bound_legs` is what prices that.
pub fn bound_join(b: Bound, other: const Bound) {
    bj_i = 0;
    while bj_i < len(other.radii) {
        bj_c = other.centres[bj_i];
        bj_r = other.radii[bj_i] ?? 0;
        if bj_c != null { bound_disc(b, Hex { q: bj_c.q, r: bj_c.r }, bj_r); }
        bj_i += 1;
    }
}

// ⚠⚠ **THE BOUND OF ONE ROUND** — the union, over the cycle's legs, of
// the disc centred on each leg's anchor with that leg's length as its
// radius (`@FR-E-Poi-Owns`).
//
// ⚠ The terminal leg is one of `c.anchors` already (R4b), so a round
// that ends somewhere it does not pass is contained without a word here
// about endings.
//
// ⚠ A routine with no cycle bounds nothing, which is the same refusal
// `cycle_build` already made and not a second one.
//
// ⚠⚠ **DISC `i` IS LEG `i`, STRUCTURALLY** — a leg with no anchor
// refuses the WHOLE bound rather than being skipped, exactly as
// `cycle_build` refuses a whole cycle for a leg that does not connect.
// ⚠ Skipping would shift every disc after it, and `bound_disc_holds`
// asks BY LEG INDEX: the claim would quietly be checked against a
// neighbouring leg's region and would read green.  `cycle_build` cannot
// produce such a leg today, which is precisely why the branch must not
// be a silent one.
//
// Enforces @FR-E-Poi-Owns: *the bound is the union over legs of the disc
// centred on each anchor with the incoming leg's length as its radius* is
// this loop and nothing else — the rule's own sentence, written once.
//
// Example: @DRY-184
pub fn bound_of(er: const Errand, c: const Cycle) -> Bound {
    bof_b = bound_new();
    if !cycle_running(c) { return bof_b; }
    bof_i = 0;
    while bof_i < len(c.anchors) {
        bof_a = c.anchors[bof_i] ?? ANCHOR_NONE;
        bof_l = c.lengths[bof_i] ?? 0;
        if bof_a == ANCHOR_NONE { return bound_new(); }
        bound_disc(bof_b, errand_anchor(er, bof_a), bof_l);
        bof_i += 1;
    }
    bof_b
}

// Is this hex inside disc `i` — the one belonging to leg `i`?
//
// ⚠⚠ **THIS IS WHERE THE CLAIM LIVES, AND `bound_holds` BELOW IS ONLY
// WHERE THE QUERY LIVES.**  What `@FR-E-Non-Increasing` proves is
// per-LEG: a body walking leg `i` is inside **that leg's** disc.  The
// union is slack — a hex outside its own leg's disc is very likely inside
// a neighbour's — so *is it in the union* is a claim almost nothing can
// falsify, and `plans/30` R5 measured that: a radius one hex short, a
// terminal leg given no disc at all, and a rim excluded by an off-by-one
// all satisfied it.
//
// ⚠⚠ **LATTICE distance, and the file header is where that is argued** —
// it is a SUPERSET of the field-distance region the proof is stated in,
// which is the safe direction and the only one.
//
// Example: @DRY-184, @DRY-185
pub fn bound_disc_holds(b: const Bound, i: integer, h: const Hex) -> boolean {
    if i < 0 || i >= len(b.radii) { return false; }
    bdh_c = b.centres[i];
    if bdh_c == null { return false; }
    lat_distance(Hex { q: bdh_c.q, r: bdh_c.r }, h) <= (b.radii[i] ?? 0)
}

// Is this hex inside the region at all?
//
// ⚠ The union, and it is `bound_disc_holds` over every disc rather than a
// second reading of the same rule — `@FR-F-Nameable-Difference` would
// have nothing to name between two copies of one comparison.
//
// Example: @DRY-184, @DRY-185
pub fn bound_holds(b: const Bound, h: const Hex) -> boolean {
    bh_i = 0;
    while bh_i < len(b.radii) {
        if bound_disc_holds(b, bh_i, h) { return true; }
        bh_i += 1;
    }
    false
}

// ⚠⚠ **THE QUERY R6 IS FOR: could anything bounded by this ever be
// inside that window?**  Two discs meet exactly when the distance
// between their centres is at most the sum of their radii — so the
// answer costs `legs` subtractions and reads no world, which is
// `@X300`'s *a query rather than a scan* made literal.
//
// ⚠ It over-answers in the same direction `bound_holds` does: *maybe*
// costs work, *no* where the truth is *yes* would be a defect.
//
// Enforces @FR-E-Boundable: *could this ever be in this window?* is a
// QUERY rather than a scan, and this is the query — `legs` subtractions,
// no world, and an answer that does not change while anything moves.
//
// Example: @DRY-190
pub fn bound_meets(b: const Bound, centre: const Hex, radius: integer) -> boolean {
    bm_i = 0;
    while bm_i < len(b.radii) {
        bm_c = b.centres[bm_i];
        bm_r = b.radii[bm_i] ?? 0;
        if bm_c != null {
            if lat_distance(Hex { q: bm_c.q, r: bm_c.r }, centre)
               <= bm_r + radius {
                return true;
            }
        }
        bm_i += 1;
    }
    false
}

// ⚠⚠ **THE POI'S OWN BOUND — the union over its ROUTES**, and the whole
// point of the file: one region for a whole population, computed once
// before anything moves.
//
// ⚠ The convenience form: it builds every route's round first, which is
// two or three flow sweeps each (`@M072`: ~500 cells).  **Never in the
// tick** — a caller that runs every tick holds the rounds itself and
// asks `poi_bound_from`.
//
// Example: @DRY-186
pub fn poi_bound(w: const PoiWorld, poi: integer, pal: vector<GroundType>,
                 pw: PaintedWorld, hl: const HeightLayer) -> Bound {
    poi_bound_from(w, poi, poi_cycles(w, pal, pw, hl))
}

// Every route's round, built ONCE.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THIS IS THE SORTIE-LONG CONSTANT, AND THE CULL TIER DEPENDS ON
// IT BEING ONE** (`plans/30` R6a).  A POI does not move and neither do
// its anchors, so a cycle is built when the sortie starts and never
// again — and if it were rebuilt each tick, a CULLED population would
// cost two flow sweeps a route and the whole saving would be spent
// working out that it could be skipped.
//
// ⚠ A vector of `Cycle` is returned rather than held in a struct
// alongside the bounds: a `vector<Struct>` whose elements carry vectors
// is [loft#974]'s shape the moment it becomes a FIELD, and a plain local
// vector is what `errand_fields` already returns for the same reason.
//
// Example: @DRY-195
pub fn poi_cycles(w: const PoiWorld, pal: vector<GroundType>,
                  pw: PaintedWorld, hl: const HeightLayer) -> vector<Cycle> {
    pcy_out: vector<Cycle> = [];
    for pcy_rt in w.routes {
        pcy_p = poi_of(w, pcy_rt.poi);
        pcy_out += [cycle_build(poi_errand(pcy_p, pcy_rt),
                                errand_role_of(pcy_rt.role), pal, pw, hl,
                                pcy_rt.kind)];
    }
    pcy_out
}

// A POI's bound, over rounds that are already built.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THE RESULT IS FOR THE QUERY AND NEVER FOR THE CLAIM.**  A POI
// with two routes concatenates two rounds' discs, so disc `i` is no
// longer leg `i` of anything — `bound_disc_holds` on THIS bound would
// ask about a leg of some other round.  ⚠ The per-leg claim belongs to
// one round's `bound_of`, which is what `tests/30_r5_the_bound.loft`
// asks; `bound_holds` and `bound_meets` are index-free and are what a
// POI's bound is for (`@X342`).
//
// Enforces @FR-E-Poi-Owns, the half `bound_of` cannot: *the POI IS the
// bound, and culling one culls its whole population*.  It ranges over
// `PoiRoute`s and can see no body at all, so the answer is one region for
// a population of any size — which is what makes it one query and not N.
//
// Example: @DRY-186
pub fn poi_bound_from(w: const PoiWorld, poi: integer,
                      cycles: vector<Cycle>) -> Bound {
    pbf_b = bound_new();
    pbf_p = poi_of(w, poi);
    if !poi_real(pbf_p) { return pbf_b; }
    pbf_i = 0;
    for pbf_rt in w.routes {
        if pbf_rt.poi == poi {
            pbf_c = cycles[pbf_i];
            if pbf_c != null {
                bound_join(pbf_b, bound_of(poi_errand(pbf_p, pbf_rt), pbf_c));
            }
        }
        pbf_i += 1;
    }
    pbf_b
}

// ─── ⚠⚠ THE THREE TIERS ────────────────────────────────────────
//
// `plans/30` R6a, `@X299`.  ⚠ Three answers to *what does this mob cost
// me this tick*, and the whole architecture is that the first one is
// asked **per POI** and the other two **per mob**:
//
//   * **CULL** — this POI's bound does not meet the window, so no mob of
//     its population can be inside it.  ⚠⚠ One `bound_meets` answers for
//     the WHOLE population, which is `@FR-E-Poi-Owns` cashed in: not one
//     `cycle_at`, not one cycle built, nothing.
//   * **EVALUATE** — inside the bound but outside the window: the mob's
//     hex is `cycle_at` at its own clock, and there is no body.
//   * **MATERIALISE** — inside the window: it needs a body, and from
//     then on `errand_step` is what moves it (`@FR-E-One-Door`).
//
// ⚠⚠ **CULL HERE MEANS *NOT MATERIALISED* AND NEVER *REMOVED***
// (`@FR-E-Poi-Persists`, `@X304`).  Nothing in this file deletes a POI
// or a `PoiMob`; a culled population is one nobody asked about this
// tick, and it is exactly where its rule says the moment anybody does.

pub const POI_TIER_CULL:        integer = 0;
pub const POI_TIER_EVALUATE:    integer = 1;
pub const POI_TIER_MATERIALISE: integer = 2;

// Can anything this bound covers be inside the window?  ⚠ The per-POI
// question, and the one that makes the rest cheap.
pub fn poi_culled(b: const Bound, player: const Hex,
                  radius: integer) -> boolean {
    !bound_meets(b, player, radius)
}

// Does this mob need a body?  ⚠ The per-MOB question, asked only of a
// POI that was not culled.
pub fn poi_tier_at(at: const Hex, player: const Hex,
                   radius: integer) -> integer {
    if lat_distance(at, player) <= radius { return POI_TIER_MATERIALISE; }
    POI_TIER_EVALUATE
}

// ─── ⚠⚠ A MOB'S OWN CLOCK ──────────────────────────────────────

// How far along the round this mob's seat puts it.
//
// ⚠⚠ **A POPULATION IS A ROTA AND THE SPACING IS DERIVED** — seat `k` of
// `count` starts `k / count` of a round further on, so forty haulers on
// one route stand forty places apart without forty authored offsets.
// ⚠ That is `@FR-E-Rule-Not-State` applied to the population: the seat
// is identity, the offset is a function of it, and nothing accumulates.
//
// ⚠ The division has a remainder and that is harmless — it decides where
// mobs stand, never whether a mob and its rule agree.  Each one's own
// clock stays exact, which is what `@M074`'s family is about.
pub fn poi_seat_offset(c: const Cycle, rate: integer, count: integer,
                       seat: integer) -> integer {
    if count <= 1 || !cycle_running(c) { return 0; }
    pso_hex = cycle_hex_units(rate);
    if pso_hex <= 0 { return 0; }
    ((seat * c.period * pso_hex) / count) ?? 0
}

// This mob's own clock at `now` — the one argument every rule about it
// takes.
//
// ⚠ `+ offset` and never `− offset`, so a rota's later seats are FURTHER
// ROUND rather than earlier in time.  ⚠⚠ `− slip` is the same
// subtraction `cycle_where` and `errand_leg` make and it means the same
// thing (`@FR-E-Slip`): a mob held up is genuinely later, so its clock
// reads earlier and its destination never changes.
pub fn poi_mob_clock(m: const PoiMob, c: const Cycle, rate: integer,
                     count: integer, now: integer) -> integer {
    now - m.slip + poi_seat_offset(c, rate, count, m.seat)
}

// The routine this mob runs — its anchors, its bag and its `slip`.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THE BAG IS DERIVED FROM THE LEG AND NEVER STORED** — an
// un-materialised mob's position comes from `cycle_at`, which asks the
// clock and the cycle, so a stored bag would be a second answer to
// *which anchor am I making for* with nothing keeping it honest.
// `errand_bag_for` is `errand_leg`'s own map read backwards.
pub fn poi_mob_errand(w: const PoiWorld, m: const PoiMob, c: const Cycle,
                      rate: integer, now: integer) -> Errand {
    pme_rt  = poi_route_of(w, m.route);
    pme_p   = poi_of(w, pme_rt.poi);
    pme_er  = poi_errand(pme_p, pme_rt);
    pme_row = errand_role_of(pme_rt.role);
    pme_t   = poi_mob_clock(m, c, rate, pme_rt.count, now);
    pme_ph  = cycle_phase(c, pme_row, rate, pme_t);
    pme_er.carry = errand_bag_for(pme_row, c.anchors[pme_ph.0] ?? ANCHOR_NONE);
    pme_er.slip  = m.slip;
    pme_er
}

// The hex this mob is standing on at `now`, body or no body.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THIS IS THE EVALUATE TIER, AND IT IS ONE `cycle_at`** — which is
// what `@X299`'s *computed, not approximated* costs, and why
// `plans/22`'s refusal of simulation LOD needs no exception.
//
// Example: @DRY-192
pub fn poi_mob_hex(w: const PoiWorld, m: const PoiMob, c: const Cycle,
                   rate: integer, now: integer) -> Hex {
    pmh_rt = poi_route_of(w, m.route);
    if m.gone || !cycle_running(c) { return Hex { q: 0, r: 0 }; }
    cycle_at(c, errand_role_of(pmh_rt.role), rate,
             poi_mob_clock(m, c, rate, pmh_rt.count, now))
}

// Has this mob's round ended, at `now`?
//
// ⚠⚠ **ASKED OF THE CLOCK AND NEVER OF A BODY** (`@FR-E-Home-Is-A-Place`).
// A population whose far half worked for ever while its near half retired
// would put the whole of R6b's equality out, and it would do it silently
// — the far half is outside the window, so nobody can see it.
// `errand_home_done` already takes a row, a routine, a rate and a hex,
// and never a roster, which is what makes this one line.
//
// Example: @DRY-193
pub fn poi_mob_done(w: const PoiWorld, m: const PoiMob, c: const Cycle,
                    rate: integer, now: integer) -> boolean {
    if m.gone { return true; }
    if !cycle_running(c) { return false; }
    pmd_rt = poi_route_of(w, m.route);
    errand_home_done(errand_role_of(pmd_rt.role),
                     poi_mob_errand(w, m, c, rate, now), rate,
                     poi_mob_hex(w, m, c, rate, now),
                     poi_mob_clock(m, c, rate, pmd_rt.count, now))
}

// ─── ⚠⚠ MATERIALISE, AND RELEASE ───────────────────────────────

// Give this mob a body, standing exactly where its rule says.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THE BODY STARTS ON THE RULE'S HEX WITH THE LEG'S BAG AND THE
// RECORD'S `slip`, and all three are needed.**  A body placed on the
// right hex with the wrong bag steers to the wrong anchor on its first
// step; one placed with `slip` of 0 has had its delay refunded; and one
// placed anywhere but `cycle_at` is `plans/30`'s invariant broken at
// site 10, which is the materialiser.
//
// ⚠ The clock the body then runs on is the RUN's `now`, and the seat
// offset is folded into `route.slip` as a NEGATIVE lateness — because
// `errand_step` and `cycle_where` read `now − slip` and there is no
// second place to put a per-mob phase.  ⚠⚠ That is the one liberty this
// function takes with `slip`'s meaning, and it is why `poi_release`
// takes the offset back out again rather than storing what it reads.
//
// ⚠⚠ **AND IT IS GIVEN THE RULE'S BANK, WHICH IS THE FOURTH THING AND
// THE ONE NOBODY WOULD THINK OF.**  `bank_new()` is a mover part-way
// through no hex at all, while the rule is generally part-way through
// one — so a body handed an empty bank releases its next hex LATE by
// exactly `cycle_carry`, every time it is materialised, and it looks
// perfect at the moment it appears.  ⚠ At the shipped robot's 1.5 hex/s
// a hex is one tick and the carry is always zero, so the whole defect is
// invisible to a regular (`@M014`'s class); a scout is what can see it.
//
// Example: @DRY-194, @DRY-198
pub fn poi_materialise(w: const PoiWorld, m: const PoiMob, c: const Cycle,
                       rate: integer, now: integer, index: integer) -> Enemy {
    pma_rt = poi_route_of(w, m.route);
    pma_at = poi_mob_hex(w, m, c, rate, now);
    pma_er = poi_mob_errand(w, m, c, rate, now);
    pma_er.slip = m.slip - poi_seat_offset(c, rate, pma_rt.count, m.seat);
    // ⚠ ONE-BASED, so `0` can go on meaning *not a POI mob at all*.
    pma_er.mob  = index + 1;
    pma_e = Enemy { q: pma_at.q, r: pma_at.r, kind: pma_rt.kind, alive: true,
                    errand: true, route: pma_er };
    bank_restore(pma_e.bank,
                 cycle_carry(rate, poi_mob_clock(m, c, rate, pma_rt.count,
                                                 now)),
                 BANK_WHOLE);
    pma_e
}

// Take this mob's body away, keeping what the body learned.
//
// ⚠⚠ **`slip` IS THE ONLY THING THAT COMES BACK, and it is the whole
// point of releasing rather than forgetting** (`@FR-E-Slip`).  The seat
// offset that `poi_materialise` folded in is taken back out, so the
// record holds a lateness and never a phase.
//
// ⚠ `m` is a PARAMETER and therefore aliases — a `PoiMob` read out of a
// vector into a local aliases too, but one RETURNED from a function is a
// copy and the write would be a silent no-op ([loft#894]).
//
// ⚠⚠ **AND THE BANK NEEDS NO FIELD, WHICH IS THE OTHER HALF OF THE SAME
// ARGUMENT.**  A body's carry at `t` is `cycle_carry(rate, t − slip)` —
// the rule's own remainder at the mob's own clock — because that is what
// `poi_materialise` gave it and `errand_step` only ever adds
// `rate × elapsed` to it.  So the carry is DERIVED at the next
// materialisation exactly as the bag is, and a `bank` on this record
// would be a second answer with nothing keeping it honest.
//
// Example: @DRY-194, @DRY-199
pub fn poi_release(w: const PoiWorld, m: PoiMob, c: const Cycle,
                   rate: integer, e: const Enemy) {
    prl_rt = poi_route_of(w, m.route);
    m.slip = e.route.slip + poi_seat_offset(c, rate, prl_rt.count, m.seat);
}

// This mob's round is over, and it never comes back.
//
// ⚠ A door of its own rather than a field somebody sets, for the reason
// `poi_state_set` is one: `gone` is what stops a retired mob being
// materialised again, and a population that quietly resurrected its
// finished half would be `@FR-E-Home-Is-A-Place` undone where nobody
// could watch it happen.
pub fn poi_retire(m: PoiMob) {
    m.gone = true;
}

// ─── ⚠⚠ THE REACH — a whole bound folded to ONE INTEGER ────────

// The furthest anything this bound covers can be from `at`.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THE CULL TEST HAS TO COST NOTHING, AND `bound_meets` IS NOT
// NOTHING** — it needs the bound, the bound needs the rounds, and the
// rounds are two flow sweeps a route.  A POI that is culled every tick
// for a whole sortie must not pay for the rounds that prove it can be.
//
// ⚠ So the bound is folded, ONCE, to a single radius about the POI's own
// hex: the union of `disc(centre_i, radius_i)` lies inside
// `disc(at, max_i(lat_distance(at, centre_i) + radius_i))`.  ⚠⚠ **A
// superset again, and the same safe direction** `bound_holds` already
// takes (`@X342`): over-answering costs work, under-answering is a
// defect.
//
// ⚠ What it buys is that the tick's per-POI question becomes **one
// subtraction** — `lat_distance(poi, player) <= reach + window` — with no
// world read and no round built.
//
// Example: @DRY-200
pub fn poi_reach(b: const Bound, at: const Hex) -> integer {
    prc_max = 0;
    prc_i = 0;
    while prc_i < len(b.radii) {
        prc_c = b.centres[prc_i];
        if prc_c != null {
            prc_d = lat_distance(Hex { q: prc_c.q, r: prc_c.r }, at)
                    + (b.radii[prc_i] ?? 0);
            if prc_d > prc_max { prc_max = prc_d; }
        }
        prc_i += 1;
    }
    prc_max
}

// Every POI's reach, built ONCE for the sortie.
//
// ⚠⚠ **A vector of INTEGERS, and that is deliberate.**  It is what a
// caller holds across ticks, and a `vector<Cycle>` or a `vector<Bound>`
// in a long-lived field is [loft#974]'s shape — *a record fetched
// through an accessor reads its vector field as EMPTY once an unrelated
// struct has been allocated*, with no diagnostic and an outcome that
// depends on allocation history rather than on the source.  ⚠ A probe of
// exactly that shape read correctly four times out of four, and the
// gotcha's own warning is that **a green reading is not evidence** — so
// the cache carries no vectors-in-structs at all.
//
// ⚠ It is safe to hold for the whole sortie because **a POI does not
// move** (`@FR-E-Place-State`) and its bound does not change with its
// state (`@X304`, gated in `tests/30_r5_the_bound.loft`).
//
// Example: @DRY-200, @DRY-205
pub fn poi_reaches(w: const PoiWorld, pal: vector<GroundType>,
                   pw: PaintedWorld, hl: const HeightLayer) -> vector<integer> {
    prs_cyc = poi_cycles(w, pal, pw, hl);
    prs_out: vector<integer> = [];
    prs_i = 0;
    while prs_i < len(w.pois) {
        prs_out += [poi_reach(poi_bound_from(w, prs_i, prs_cyc),
                              poi_hex(poi_of(w, prs_i)))];
        prs_i += 1;
    }
    prs_out
}

// ─── ⚠⚠ THE SURVEY — what a POI costs this tick ────────────────

// What one POI cost, and what it produced.
//
// ⚠⚠ **`asked` IS THE COST GATE, AND IT IS A COUNT AND NEVER A CLOCK**
// (`@M029`: two identical calls differed 5.4x).  One mob asked is one
// closed-form evaluation — O(legs), a modulo and an index
// (`@FR-E-Closed-Form`) — so counting mobs counts the work exactly, and
// counting milliseconds would only measure the box.
//
// ⚠ Every field is an integer, so this struct is safe to hold and to
// return; nothing here carries a vector ([loft#974]).
pub struct PoiSurvey {
    tier:  integer,   // the POI's own tier — CULL, or the best of its mobs
    live:  integer,   // mobs that need a body
    seen:  integer,   // mobs computed, and not drawn
    done:  integer,   // mobs whose round has ended
    asked: integer,   // ⚠ mobs evaluated — THE COST
}

pub fn poi_survey_new() -> PoiSurvey {
    PoiSurvey { tier: POI_TIER_CULL, live: 0, seen: 0, done: 0, asked: 0 }
}

// ⚠⚠ **THE THREE TIERS, APPLIED — and the shape of this function IS the
// architecture.**  The cull test is asked ONCE, before the loop, and it
// is the only thing a distant POI ever costs: `bound_meets` over `legs`
// discs, no world read, no mob touched.  Everything after it is per mob.
//
// ⚠ It answers rather than acts — no body is made here and nothing is
// removed — so `plans/30` § Count the RE-ASSERTION SITES gains no site
// from it.  R6b is where the answer is spent.
//
// ⚠⚠ **A mob whose round has ENDED is counted and never evaluated
// again** — the ending is on its own clock (`poi_mob_done`), so a
// population's far half retires exactly when its near half would have,
// which is what R6b's equality needs and what nobody could see if it
// were wrong.
//
// Example: @DRY-196
pub fn poi_survey(w: const PoiWorld, poi: integer, b: const Bound,
                  cycles: vector<Cycle>, player: const Hex,
                  radius: integer, now: integer) -> PoiSurvey {
    psv_out = poi_survey_new();
    if poi_culled(b, player, radius) { return psv_out; }
    psv_out.tier = POI_TIER_EVALUATE;
    for psv_m in w.mobs {
        psv_rt = poi_route_of(w, psv_m.route);
        if psv_rt.poi != poi { continue; }
        psv_c = cycles[psv_m.route];
        if psv_c == null { continue; }
        psv_rate = bank_rate(enemy_speed(psv_rt.kind));
        psv_out.asked += 1;
        if poi_mob_done(w, psv_m, psv_c, psv_rate, now) {
            psv_out.done += 1;
            continue;
        }
        if poi_tier_at(poi_mob_hex(w, psv_m, psv_c, psv_rate, now), player,
                       radius) == POI_TIER_MATERIALISE {
            psv_out.live += 1;
            psv_out.tier  = POI_TIER_MATERIALISE;
            continue;
        }
        psv_out.seen += 1;
    }
    psv_out
}

// ─── ⚠⚠ THE STEP — the tiers, SPENT ────────────────────────────

// How far from the player a mob has to be before it needs a body.
//
// ⚠⚠ **The scrambler bubble's own 25 hexes, and it is the same number
// for a reason rather than by coincidence.**  The bubble is where an
// ambient robot stops being ambient (`wave_cutoff`), so a mob that could
// cross it must already be a body — a robot that entered the bubble
// while still a rule would be cut off by a sweep that cannot see it.
// ⚠ Anything LARGER is free correctness and costs work; anything smaller
// is a defect.  Equal is the smallest safe number, which is why it is
// the one.
pub const POI_WINDOW_HEXES: integer = 25;

// Which roster slot, if any, holds this mob's body.
//
// ⚠ A sweep of the roster and never an index kept the other way round:
// the roster is rebuilt by `errand_depart` and read by everything, so a
// forward index would be silently invalidated (`plans/30` § Count the
// RE-ASSERTION SITES, site 6).
//
// ⚠⚠ **A DEAD ROBOT IS NOT A BODY THIS POI OWNS.**  `wave_deaths` MARKS
// rather than removes — the corpse stays on the roster and raises rubble,
// because *bodies are terrain* — so a sweep that did not ask `alive`
// would report a body for ever and the record would never learn that the
// player killed it.  ⚠ That is exactly the state `live` exists to name:
// the roster's *no body here* means both *we never made one* and *the one
// we made is dead*, and only the record tells them apart.
//
// Example: @DRY-207
fn poi_body_of(state: const WaveState, index: integer) -> integer {
    pbo_i = 0;
    for pbo_e in state.enemies {
        if pbo_e.route.mob == index + 1 && pbo_e.alive { return pbo_i; }
        pbo_i += 1;
    }
    0 - 1
}

// ⚠⚠ **THE MATERIALISER — site 10 of `plans/30` § Count the
// RE-ASSERTION SITES, and the one that puts a body somewhere.**
//
// ⚠ It runs at the TOP of the game's step, immediately before
// `wave_tick`, for exactly the reason `errand_depart` runs at the top of
// the tick: a body made now must be one this tick's fields, occupancy
// and move order already know about, and one taken away now must be gone
// before anything surveys the roster.
//
// ⚠⚠ **A POI IS CULLED BY ONE SUBTRACTION** — `lat_distance(poi, player)
// against `reach + window` — so a distant POI costs no round, no
// `cycle_at` and no roster sweep.  The rounds are built only for the
// POIs that survive that test, which is what makes `@FR-E-Poi-Owns` a
// saving in the TICK rather than only in `poi_survey`.
//
// ⚠ Answers (bodies made, bodies taken back, mobs retired, **mobs
// LOOKED AT**).
//
// ⚠⚠ **The last one is the cost, and it is a COUNT and never a clock**
// (`@M029`) — the same reading `PoiSurvey.asked` carries one function
// over.  ⚠ It is what makes the cull OBSERVABLE: without it a culled POI
// and a surveyed one both make no bodies, so a gate could only see the
// saving by timing it (`@M082`).
//
// Example: @DRY-201, @DRY-202, @DRY-203, @DRY-204
pub fn poi_step(state: WaveState, pal: vector<GroundType>, pw: PaintedWorld,
                reach: vector<integer>, window: integer) -> (integer,
                                                             integer,
                                                             integer,
                                                             integer) {
    pst_made = 0;
    pst_back = 0;
    pst_gone = 0;
    pst_seen = 0;
    if len(state.pois.mobs) == 0 { return (0, 0, 0, 0); }
    pst_at = Hex { q: state.player.q, r: state.player.r };
    pst_p  = 0;
    while pst_p < len(state.pois.pois) {
        pst_poi = poi_of(state.pois, pst_p);
        pst_r   = reach[pst_p] ?? 0;
        // ⚠⚠ **THE CULL, and it is the whole architecture in one line.**
        if lat_distance(poi_hex(pst_poi), pst_at) > pst_r + window {
            pst_p += 1;
            continue;
        }
        // ⚠ The rounds, built only now — for a POI near enough to matter.
        pst_cyc = poi_cycles(state.pois, pal, pw, state.heights);
        pst_i = 0;
        while pst_i < len(state.pois.mobs) {
            pst_m = state.pois.mobs[pst_i];
            if pst_m == null { pst_i += 1; continue; }
            pst_rt = poi_route_of(state.pois, pst_m.route);
            if pst_rt.poi != pst_p || pst_m.gone { pst_i += 1; continue; }
            pst_c = pst_cyc[pst_m.route];
            if pst_c == null { pst_i += 1; continue; }
            pst_rate = bank_rate(enemy_speed(pst_rt.kind));
            pst_slot = poi_body_of(state, pst_i);
            pst_seen += 1;

            // ⚠⚠ **A BODY THAT VANISHED, or one the BUBBLE took.**  The
            // roster is the record of who is materialised, and the one
            // thing it cannot say is which of *never made* and *made and
            // lost* it means — which is what `live` is for.  ⚠ Either
            // way the mob leaves this POI's population: it died, or it
            // went through the one door and is one of the wave now.
            if pst_m.live {
                if pst_slot < 0 {
                    poi_retire(pst_m);
                    pst_gone += 1;
                    pst_i += 1;
                    continue;
                }
                pst_body = state.enemies[pst_slot];
                if pst_body != null {
                    if !pst_body.errand {
                        poi_retire(pst_m);
                        pst_gone += 1;
                        pst_i += 1;
                        continue;
                    }
                }
            }

            // ⚠⚠ **THE ROUND'S OWN ENDING, asked of the CLOCK** — a
            // population whose far half worked for ever while its near
            // half retired would break the equality this phase gates,
            // and nobody could see it.  ⚠ `errand_depart` takes the BODY
            // at the top of the tick; this takes the RECORD, so the two
            // agree by asking the same function.
            if poi_mob_done(state.pois, pst_m, pst_c, pst_rate, state.now) {
                poi_retire(pst_m);
                pst_gone += 1;
                pst_i += 1;
                continue;
            }

            pst_want = poi_tier_at(poi_mob_hex(state.pois, pst_m, pst_c,
                                               pst_rate, state.now),
                                   pst_at, window);
            if pst_want == POI_TIER_MATERIALISE && pst_slot < 0 {
                state.enemies += [poi_materialise(state.pois, pst_m, pst_c,
                                                  pst_rate, state.now,
                                                  pst_i)];
                pst_m.live = true;
                pst_made += 1;
            }
            if pst_want == POI_TIER_EVALUATE && pst_slot >= 0 {
                pst_e = state.enemies[pst_slot];
                if pst_e != null {
                    poi_release(state.pois, pst_m, pst_c, pst_rate, pst_e);
                }
                poi_drop_body(state, pst_slot);
                pst_m.live = false;
                pst_back += 1;
            }
            pst_i += 1;
        }
        pst_p += 1;
    }
    (pst_made, pst_back, pst_gone, pst_seen)
}

// Take one body off the roster.
//
// ⚠ Rebuilt rather than spliced, which is `errand_depart`'s own shape —
// there is no vector remove, and a copy is what the roster already pays
// for a departure.
fn poi_drop_body(state: WaveState, slot: integer) {
    pdb_keep: vector<Enemy> = [];
    pdb_i = 0;
    for pdb_e in state.enemies {
        if pdb_i != slot { pdb_keep += [pdb_e]; }
        pdb_i += 1;
    }
    state.enemies = pdb_keep;
}

// ─── ⚠ WHAT IS REFUSED ──────────────────────────────────────────

// Why this route is not a route, or "".
//
// ⚠⚠ **THE ONE THAT MATTERS IS THE LAST: a route may name a POI its
// round never visits.**  `poi_errand` would then put the POI's hex into
// an anchor nothing reads, the mobs would walk a round the place is not
// on, and *the POI is the bound* would be false with every count green.
// ⚠ It is R4b's lesson with a new subject — a row can promise something
// its geometry cannot keep, and the refusal belongs where the geometry
// is known.
//
// Example: @DRY-189
pub fn poi_route_fault(w: const PoiWorld, rt: const PoiRoute) -> text {
    if rt.poi < 0 || rt.poi >= len(w.pois) {
        return "route names POI {rt.poi}, and this world has {len(w.pois)}";
    }
    prf_p = poi_of(w, rt.poi);
    if !poi_real(prf_p) {
        return "route is attached to a POI of kind {prf_p.kind}, which is "
               + "not a place — its population would have no anchor";
    }
    prf_row = errand_role_of(rt.role);
    if prf_row.empty == ANCHOR_NONE {
        return "route runs role {rt.role}, which has no round to walk";
    }
    if rt.count <= 0 {
        return "route has a population of {rt.count}";
    }
    prf_mine = poi_kind_of(prf_p.kind).anchor;
    if !poi_role_uses(prf_row, prf_mine) {
        return "a '{poi_kind_of(prf_p.kind).name}' is the "
               + "{prf_mine} anchor, and role '{prf_row.name}' never goes "
               + "there — so this population would not be bounded by the "
               + "place it is attached to";
    }
    ""
}

// Why this world is not one, or "".  ⚠ The first failing route names
// itself, because a message that said *some route* would send a reader
// back through the list by hand.
pub fn poi_world_fault(w: const PoiWorld) -> text {
    pwf_i = 0;
    for pwf_rt in w.routes {
        pwf_why = poi_route_fault(w, pwf_rt);
        if pwf_why != "" { return "routes[{pwf_i}]: {pwf_why}"; }
        pwf_i += 1;
    }
    ""
}
